Dagu host regression tests
==========================

These tests compile functions extracted from the kernel sources against small
userspace substitutes. They need Python 3, a native C compiler with sanitizer
support, and pthreads, plus make and tar. The legacy kselftest framework
also needs bc and rsync. They do not require root or access to device sysfs.
They do not validate kernel RCU, IPC priority restoration, reclaim progress,
boot, OOM behavior or device performance.

Run from the kernel source root:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu run_tests

SANITIZERS defaults to undefined. Use SANITIZERS=address,undefined to also
check memory safety on hosts supporting AddressSanitizer. HOSTCC selects
the native compiler used for the extracted code.

Use a separate build directory with:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu O=/tmp/dagu-build run_tests

The standard kselftest install target bundles the tested source files in
kernel-sources.tar, so installed tests do not need the original checkout:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu install INSTALL_PATH=/tmp/dagu-tests
  /tmp/dagu-tests/run_kselftest.sh

Build the bundle again after changing a source file before installing tests.
In-tree runs read the live checkout; installed runs read their source bundle.
The shared extractor handles braces inside C strings, character literals and
comments, but is not a general-purpose C parser.

This kernel's legacy TAP runner reports failures as "not ok ... [FAIL]"
without setting a failing exit status. Consumers must check the TAP results;
the Dagu host checks workflow does so explicitly.
